@startuml Web_Identity_Federation_Cognito
title Web Identity Federation: Google -> Cognito User Pool -> Cognito Identity Pool -> STS -> DynamoDB\n(Example: bob@gmail.com, region us-east-1, account 111122223333)

autonumber
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign left
skinparam responseMessageBelowArrow true

actor "Bob\n(Mobile App user)" as User
participant "Mobile App\n(OrdersApp)" as App
participant "Google\n(OIDC IdP)\naccounts.google.com" as Google

box "AWS Account 111122223333 (us-east-1)" #FFF4E5
  participant "Cognito USER POOL\nus-east-1_AbC123\n(AUTHENTICATION:\nwho are you?)" as UP
  participant "Cognito IDENTITY POOL\nus-east-1:1111-2222...\n(AUTHORIZATION to AWS:\nwhich AWS creds?)" as IdP
  participant "AWS STS" as STS
  participant "IAM Role\nrole/Cognito_OrdersAuth_Role" as Role
  database "DynamoDB\nOrders table" as DDB
end box

== One-time setup (admins) ==
Google -> Google : Register OAuth client in Google Cloud Console\n-> Google client ID + secret
Google -> UP : Add Google as federated IdP in the USER POOL\n(paste Google client ID + secret here)
note over UP
  App client in user pool:
    client ID = 4abc123userpoolclient
  The app only knows THIS client ID,
  not the Google one.
end note
UP -> IdP : Add the user pool as an authentication provider\nin the IDENTITY POOL (user pool ID + app client ID)
IdP -> Role : Map authenticated users -> Cognito_OrdersAuth_Role
note over Role
  Trust policy:
  {
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Principal": { "Federated": "cognito-identity.amazonaws.com" },
    "Action": "sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity",
    "Condition": {
      "StringEquals": {
        "cognito-identity.amazonaws.com:aud": "us-east-1:1111-2222..." },
      "ForAnyValue:StringLike": {
        "cognito-identity.amazonaws.com:amr": "authenticated" } }
  }
  Permissions policy:
    dynamodb:Query on table/Orders
end note

== Stage 1: AUTHENTICATION (User Pool) ==
User -> App : Tap "Sign in with Google"
App -> UP : Open user pool Hosted UI\n/oauth2/authorize?identity_provider=Google\n&client_id=4abc123userpoolclient
UP --> User : Redirect to Google sign-in
User -> Google : Enter bob@gmail.com + password
note right of Google
  Password is checked ONLY by Google.
  AWS never sees it.
end note
Google --> UP : Redirect back with Google authorization code\n(to user pool /oauth2/idpresponse)
UP -> Google : Exchange code for Google ID token (JWT)
Google --> UP : Google ID token
UP -> UP : Validate Google token:\nsignature (Google JWKS), iss, exp,\naud = Google client ID
UP -> UP : Create / link user profile in the pool\n(e.g. Google_10987654321)
UP --> App : Authorization code (redirect to app)
App -> UP : POST /oauth2/token (exchange code)
UP --> App : USER POOL tokens (JWTs signed by the user pool):\nID token, Access token, Refresh token
note right of App
  The app now holds a COGNITO token,
  not the raw Google token.
  A JWT proves identity, but it
  CANNOT sign AWS API calls.
end note

== Stage 2: AUTHORIZATION to AWS (Identity Pool) ==
App -> IdP : GetId(IdentityPoolId,\n  Logins = { "cognito-idp.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/us-east-1_AbC123":\n            <user pool ID token> })
IdP -> IdP : Validate user pool ID token:\nsignature (user pool JWKS), iss = this user pool,\naud = 4abc123userpoolclient, not expired
IdP --> App : IdentityId (stable ID for Bob)
App -> IdP : GetCredentialsForIdentity(IdentityId, Logins = {...same token...})
IdP -> STS : AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity(\n  RoleArn = ...:role/Cognito_OrdersAuth_Role,\n  WebIdentityToken = <Cognito-issued OIDC token> )
STS -> Role : Check trust policy:\nFederated = cognito-identity.amazonaws.com?\naud = this identity pool? amr = authenticated?
Role --> STS : Allowed
STS --> IdP : Temporary credentials
IdP --> App : AccessKeyId, SecretAccessKey,\nSessionToken, Expiration (~1 hour)

== Stage 3: Call AWS ==
App -> DDB : Query Orders table\n(SigV4-signed with temporary credentials)
DDB -> Role : Evaluate role PERMISSIONS policy
Role --> DDB : dynamodb:Query allowed
DDB --> App : Bob's orders
App --> User : Show orders

note over User, DDB
  Who does what:
  - Google: authenticates Bob (checks password), issues Google JWT
  - User Pool: validates Google token, issues ITS OWN JWTs (the "receptionist")
  - Identity Pool: trusts the user pool, swaps its JWT for AWS creds (the "badge office")
  - STS: validates trust policy (aud = identity pool, amr = authenticated), mints temp creds
  - IAM Role: trust policy = WHO can assume; permissions policy = WHAT they can do
  Exam tip: SAML -> AssumeRoleWithSAML | OIDC / Cognito -> AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity
end note

@enduml
